<p>Pope Leo XIV used the second leg of his Africa tour on Wednesday to renew his calls for peace, dialogue and coexistence, after President Trump again criticized him on social media over Iran and other issues. Speaking while traveling from Algeria to Cameroon, the first U.S.-born pope said the world needs a message of peace and respect across differences, and that his travels had underscored the value of dialogue between communities. The dispute has drawn unusual attention because it sets a sitting American president against the first U.S.-born pope.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/XkZJsi3c8D8AcPNqe6oXwH4kV1czsq1543qHRg--7Iu5WcsCH61y3BLWcf5LFKztUCwlpdmk8w6RURl_nHQviKMIKEe5CprrjegwHGY9qmnen0kx2-1FB3794W_AFLJe75cgWk-zyhdkWHPa9WZ6R0Z618wn-Znt6Cw2w0De2c3LtCS7A7JgHXErsip5vXbVE1s2Wg1L1k0GW0RxkVb2BfTaQaxU1-D23fNDjfFB0dc0YXBY6PLVoku6CG1DdtNK79fMhTUO_NOExMMCKQ' alt='Pope Leo XIV after arriving at Yaounde Nsimalen International Airport amid his apostolic journey in Yaounde, Cameroon on Wednesday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Peace warning: The Globe and Mail reported that Leo said the “rich and powerful” threaten peace, sharpening his message beyond a broader appeal for coexistence.</li><li>Conservative support: The New York Times reported that, unlike his predecessor, Leo is drawing support from a broad swath of conservative Catholics, giving him a different kind of political standing in debates with President Trump.</li><li>Vatican reaction: Visitors in St. Peter’s Square told ABC News that Trump’s criticism of a pope preaching peace was inappropriate, adding a lay Catholic response from inside the Vatican itself.</li><li>Democracy warning: Dawn reported that Leo warned of democracies sliding into “majoritarian tyranny,” framing his critique as concern about how power is used rather than a party-political argument.</li></ul><blockquote>Although we have different beliefs, we have different languages, we have different social positions, every one of us has a dignity that has been given by God - Pope Leo XIV</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Trump: Trump tied his criticism of Leo to Iran, saying the pope should be told about the killing of protesters there and arguing that Iran must not obtain a nuclear bomb. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1991929/trump-doubles-down-in-criticising-pope-leo-over-iran'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Pope Leo XIV: Leo said his travels in Africa had already shown how important dialogue between different communities can be, tying his peace appeal to what he was seeing on the trip rather than only to his dispute with Washington. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/africa/article/3350217/pope-leo-stresses-message-peace-and-unity-despite-another-trump-attack'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>African Catholics: Catholics interviewed in Africa said Trump’s public spat with Leo was startling, given earlier frustrations over remarks about African countries and cuts to foreign assistance. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/unthinkable-african-catholics-recoil-at-trumps-spat-with-leo'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva: Brazil’s president said Catholics should defend Leo from attacks by “powerful people,” turning the dispute into a broader political and moral show of support for the pontiff. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/brazils-lula-defends-pope-leo-message-catholics-after-132075133'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>Religious scholars: Experts told NPR that although popes have often weighed in on public affairs, Trump’s direct personal attacks on Leo mark a different kind of confrontation between the White House and the Vatican. (<a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/15/nx-s1-5779690/pope-leo-donald-trump-war-iran-vance-history'>NPR</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/15/nx-s1-5779690/pope-leo-donald-trump-war-iran-vance-history'>Popes have spoken out on politics before. But with Trump and P